WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:02.540
All right, welcome back.

2
00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:05.879
We're here for ICPFX camera hardware configuration.

3
00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:07.799
We've got a number of slides to go through

4
00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:10.519
and then we're going to show you the camera

5
00:00:10.519 --> 00:00:12.800
and its various components and elements.

6
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.359
And we'll get started with talking

7
00:00:15.359 --> 00:00:17.719
about the different cameras that we have available

8
00:00:17.719 --> 00:00:22.239
at the NAND stage, the LA R&D stage,

9
00:00:22.239 --> 00:00:24.920
and here at the Latte stage in Culver City.

10
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.260
We normally like to classify cameras

11
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.879
into a couple of different groups.

12
00:00:29.879 --> 00:00:32.600
There are high-end cameras, mid-range cameras,

13
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.399
and then low-end cameras that can work really well

14
00:00:35.399 --> 00:00:37.280
for ICPFX.

15
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.979
At the NAND stage and at the LA R&D stage,

16
00:00:39.979 --> 00:00:43.060
we use the Arri Alexa Mini LF camera.

17
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.979
This is a full-frame camera with a 1.0 crop factor,

18
00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:49.159
so no crop factor at all.

19
00:00:49.159 --> 00:00:51.020
It does implement a rolling shutter,

20
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.600
which is something that we're going to talk about

21
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.280
more in detail today.

22
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.079
And it is a fairly expensive camera,

23
00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:00.320
about $60,000 for the body, and fully decked out.

24
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.520
It can run up into six figures.

25
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.280
So if you're serious, this is definitely the camera

26
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.879
to utilize for your higher-end productions.

27
00:01:08.879 --> 00:01:13.879
For mid-range, we have the Blackmagic Ursa Mini G2,

28
00:01:13.879 --> 00:01:15.959
generation two camera.

29
00:01:15.959 --> 00:01:19.079
This is a super 35 millimeter sensor.

30
00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:22.519
It does have a crop factor of about 1.5.

31
00:01:22.519 --> 00:01:25.439
It's also a rolling shutter camera,

32
00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:28.920
and it's about six grand for the body itself.

33
00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.560
This is what we use here at the Latte Lab,

34
00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.459
and a couple of other facilities

35
00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:35.939
also have similar cameras to this one.

36
00:01:35.939 --> 00:01:38.659
On the low end, you can deploy things

37
00:01:38.659 --> 00:01:42.120
like the Panasonic BGH1, which I have here on the desk.

38
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.079
This is a Micro Four Thirds camera.

39
00:01:46.079 --> 00:01:49.239
It is really good for broadcast situations

40
00:01:49.239 --> 00:01:54.239
because it does and has genlock capabilities on the back,

41
00:01:55.239 --> 00:02:00.239
BNC ports for SDI output, for timecode, and for genlock.

42
00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:03.000
However, according to the manual,

43
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.239
it does have some issues with genlocking to 24p.

44
00:02:06.239 --> 00:02:11.240
So if you're going to be using the BGH1 or the BS1H,

45
00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:13.919
which is its full-frame counterpart,

46
00:02:13.919 --> 00:02:16.680
you might wanna be in more of a broadcast situation

47
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.680
where you're operating at 2997 or at 60 frames per second.

48
00:02:22.379 --> 00:02:24.240
But it's a great low-cost camera.

49
00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.439
It's about $2,000 for the body

50
00:02:26.439 --> 00:02:29.340
and about $1,000 for the lens

51
00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.340
and other particular extra devices

52
00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.460
that you might wanna include,

53
00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:37.719
like the Video Assist and wireless transmission

54
00:02:37.719 --> 00:02:38.800
or what else.

55
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.719
Now, when you're selecting a camera,

56
00:02:40.719 --> 00:02:43.319
there are some things that are good to have,

57
00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:45.740
and then there's some things you might wanna avoid.

58
00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.000
So let's take a look at the list for good to have.

59
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.439
Number one, higher than average resolution

60
00:02:50.439 --> 00:02:52.159
for its sensor class.

61
00:02:52.159 --> 00:02:55.639
Essentially, the higher the resolution that you have

62
00:02:55.639 --> 00:02:58.599
for your camera that you're gonna be using on stage,

63
00:02:58.599 --> 00:02:59.759
probably the better.

64
00:02:59.759 --> 00:03:00.580
Why?

65
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.840
Because it helps with the mitigation of Moir.

66
00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.400
It's something that is gonna be really hard

67
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:07.300
to get rid of.

68
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:11.199
However, the LED panels only has,

69
00:03:11.199 --> 00:03:12.860
they have a certain amount of resolution

70
00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.009
and a certain dot pitch.

71
00:03:15.009 --> 00:03:16.949
And if you can have a camera with a sensor

72
00:03:16.949 --> 00:03:19.270
that's a higher resolution,

73
00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.990
that'll help to mitigate that just a little bit.

74
00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.750
You should pick and choose cameras

75
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.789
that implement a fast rolling shutter

76
00:03:27.789 --> 00:03:30.389
or even better, a global shutter.

77
00:03:30.389 --> 00:03:31.949
And we're gonna talk about the differences

78
00:03:31.949 --> 00:03:34.909
of those two later on in this class.

79
00:03:34.909 --> 00:03:37.550
But fast rolling shutters are fine

80
00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.909
if you're planning on utilizing genlock,

81
00:03:40.909 --> 00:03:42.229
which you're gonna need.

82
00:03:42.229 --> 00:03:45.569
But global shutters will also give you

83
00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:46.710
a much better result.

84
00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.960
And we'll talk about why that is in just a bit.

85
00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:53.080
There's cameras with optical low pass filters.

86
00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:56.039
Now this is a way to place a filter

87
00:03:56.039 --> 00:03:58.360
in front of the actual sensor itself.

88
00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.479
And it kind of slightly softens the image

89
00:04:01.479 --> 00:04:03.919
just a little bit so that it helps

90
00:04:03.919 --> 00:04:08.319
to reduce aliasing problems and Moir as well.

91
00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:09.599
You wanna make sure your camera

92
00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:14.599
has really low latency SDI outputs.

93
00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:16.959
You know, anything that adds to latency

94
00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.160
is going to be something that you're gonna detect

95
00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:21.800
once we get through the entire signal flow

96
00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.079
of the ICV effects volume.

97
00:04:24.079 --> 00:04:26.240
So you wanna make sure that the output

98
00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:30.360
from your camera's SDI port is fast.

99
00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.399
Next, cameras should have timecode BNC connectors

100
00:04:34.399 --> 00:04:37.000
along with genlock BNC connectors.

101
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.399
There's a way to get around this a little bit,

102
00:04:39.399 --> 00:04:42.420
but having a dedicated BNC port

103
00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:46.920
on the back of your camera is the way to go for sure.

104
00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.920
And then I recommend that you utilize manual prime lenses

105
00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.160
rather than fly-by-wire lenses

106
00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.480
or something that is more electronically managed.

107
00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.360
It's only for the benefit of being able

108
00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.360
to have more direct control over your lenses.

109
00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.360
Now, if you do have a follow focus

110
00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:11.199
or some type of device that's attached to the camera,

111
00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:12.500
that can stand in for that.

112
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.000
But normally we like to say to folks,

113
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.720
use a manual prime lens, it's a better option for you.

114
00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:22.560
Now, what you might wanna avoid on the other hand,

115
00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.199
are cameras with limited ISO and low dynamic range.

116
00:05:26.199 --> 00:05:28.439
You're gonna be shooting the LED wall

117
00:05:28.439 --> 00:05:31.319
and you're gonna have a lot of sensitive color science

118
00:05:31.319 --> 00:05:32.620
to have to deal with.

119
00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.120
So you wanna make sure you have a camera

120
00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:38.759
that has a high number of options for its ISO

121
00:05:38.759 --> 00:05:41.560
and its light sensitivity

122
00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.279
and having a higher amount of dynamic range

123
00:05:45.279 --> 00:05:46.800
and a greater options in there

124
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.319
will also afford you more choices

125
00:05:51.319 --> 00:05:54.920
when shooting on location and at your stage.

126
00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.079
Autofocus solutions,

127
00:05:57.079 --> 00:05:59.259
not normally something that we recommend

128
00:05:59.259 --> 00:06:01.920
simply because they can sometimes choose

129
00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.319
to autofocus onto the actual wall itself

130
00:06:05.319 --> 00:06:09.800
and then that can become problematic in seeing more.

131
00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:12.040
So again, this is why we say,

132
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:15.720
let's go ahead and use manual focus lenses instead.

133
00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.879
Signal converters, these are devices

134
00:06:17.879 --> 00:06:20.779
that attach to your camera to convert the signal

135
00:06:20.779 --> 00:06:25.779
from either HDMI to SDI or SDI to HDMI, et cetera.

136
00:06:25.879 --> 00:06:30.279
These signal converters are not really desirable

137
00:06:30.279 --> 00:06:34.019
because they will take even the shortest amount of time

138
00:06:34.019 --> 00:06:35.319
to convert that signal,

139
00:06:35.319 --> 00:06:39.339
which will unfortunately add to additional latency.

140
00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:43.199
So try to avoid signal converters if you can.

141
00:06:43.199 --> 00:06:45.819
Next, anamorphic lenses.

142
00:06:45.819 --> 00:06:48.800
Anamorphic lenses are really desirable

143
00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:49.839
by a lot of folks.

144
00:06:49.839 --> 00:06:53.240
However, they can cause some issues

145
00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:55.360
in the ICV effects process.

146
00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:57.980
They are being worked on and being included

147
00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:01.040
in upcoming releases of the engine.

148
00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:02.600
And once that happens,

149
00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.560
support for anamorphic lenses will be something

150
00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:08.199
that we won't have to worry about anymore.

151
00:07:08.199 --> 00:07:10.920
Finally, variable aperture lenses.

152
00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:14.899
This is something that you can get for a lens

153
00:07:14.899 --> 00:07:18.589
that will actually adjust its aperture

154
00:07:18.589 --> 00:07:20.589
depending upon the lighting conditions.

155
00:07:20.589 --> 00:07:24.589
And this is something that I would probably suggest

156
00:07:24.589 --> 00:07:29.689
that you avoid simply because depending upon

157
00:07:29.689 --> 00:07:32.350
those lighting conditions, depending upon your focal length,

158
00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:34.550
depending upon your aperture setting,

159
00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.129
you don't want this changing depending upon

160
00:07:37.129 --> 00:07:38.629
what you're trying to focus in on.

161
00:07:38.629 --> 00:07:43.189
So try to avoid variable aperture lenses

162
00:07:43.189 --> 00:07:47.870
and stick again with primes.

163
00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:51.110
Okay, now there are a number of different sensors

164
00:07:51.110 --> 00:07:52.930
that you can find in the various cameras

165
00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:54.899
that you're going to be using.

166
00:07:54.899 --> 00:07:56.459
They're all basically based off

167
00:07:56.459 --> 00:07:59.620
of the 35 millimeter film standard.

168
00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:03.220
If we use that as kind of like what our goalpost is,

169
00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.019
you can see that there are a number

170
00:08:05.019 --> 00:08:07.379
of different sensor sizes and types.

171
00:08:07.379 --> 00:08:12.220
You have two thirds inch sensor, you have micro four thirds,

172
00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:17.250
you have APS-C, you have super 35 and full frame.

173
00:08:17.529 --> 00:08:22.019
Full frame basically is the same dimensions

174
00:08:22.019 --> 00:08:25.519
and shooting area as what you're afforded by

175
00:08:25.519 --> 00:08:27.560
by a 35 millimeter film.

176
00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:32.039
And it's probably your most optimal choice,

177
00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:35.519
but super 35 is also a good alternative.

178
00:08:35.519 --> 00:08:37.200
Even though it does introduce a little bit

179
00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.279
of a crop factor, it's slightly smaller lens size.

180
00:08:42.279 --> 00:08:45.320
If you have a camera that has a rolling shutter,

181
00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:46.840
it should be a little faster then,

182
00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.500
which should prevent some of those issues

183
00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.379
that might occur with flickering.

184
00:08:51.379 --> 00:08:54.480
But when you're working on a professional stage,

185
00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:58.580
super 35 or full frame are probably the two choices

186
00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.220
that I would recommend.

187
00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:01.879
If you are going to be working on more

188
00:09:01.879 --> 00:09:05.240
of an independent based indie production,

189
00:09:06.159 --> 00:09:10.360
the using a micro four thirds or APS-C cameras,

190
00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.940
totally a fine choice, especially for broadcast.

191
00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:18.039
Now, when it comes to the various hardware configurations,

192
00:09:18.039 --> 00:09:20.000
you're gonna see that Unreal Engine of course,

193
00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.039
has a virtual camera, that virtual camera

194
00:09:24.039 --> 00:09:27.080
and it's a hardware setting configuration

195
00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:29.840
for the camera back should match

196
00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:33.879
the sensor size dimensions of the camera you're using.

197
00:09:33.879 --> 00:09:36.840
So always check this to make sure,

198
00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:39.960
if you don't, you'll have a slight mismatch

199
00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:44.960
and that mismatch will potentially cause you issues

200
00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:48.639
or problems with your field of view.

201
00:09:48.639 --> 00:09:51.600
So double check this and make sure

202
00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.720
that the sensor size of your camera

203
00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:57.039
is entered into the Unreal camera film back.

204
00:09:57.039 --> 00:10:00.159
Now, we're gonna talk about building your own camera rig

205
00:10:00.159 --> 00:10:02.940
and looking at some of the pieces of equipment

206
00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:04.460
of a good camera rig.

207
00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:06.620
Well, when talking about building your own camera rig,

208
00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:09.820
we're gonna first take a look at the tripod.

209
00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:11.059
If you look at the tripod,

210
00:10:11.059 --> 00:10:14.740
you wanna have a really nice high quality tripod

211
00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:19.340
that is stabilized preferably with a nice set of wheels

212
00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:21.200
on the bottom so that you can move your camera

213
00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:22.559
throughout your stage.

214
00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:25.500
This particular tripod,

215
00:10:29.830 --> 00:10:32.429
the big advantage is that you do have

216
00:10:32.429 --> 00:10:34.870
various leveling bubbles on the tripod,

217
00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:37.309
to make sure that your camera is level

218
00:10:37.309 --> 00:10:42.259
and it gives you a solid base to work on.

219
00:10:42.259 --> 00:10:43.559
You also wanna make sure that you have

220
00:10:43.559 --> 00:10:47.580
a good video head as well, a fluid video head.

221
00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:50.200
This is basically the,

222
00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:53.460
it'll give you options to make sure

223
00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:57.460
that when you're moving your camera, it's nice and smooth.

224
00:10:57.460 --> 00:10:59.679
You can lock off the various accesses

225
00:10:59.679 --> 00:11:04.100
so that you can lock off pan and or tilt

226
00:11:04.659 --> 00:11:07.820
with these particular locking mechanisms.

227
00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:12.179
Works really well and I'd recommend that

228
00:11:12.179 --> 00:11:14.700
if you spend a little bit more on the tripod,

229
00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.409
you'll appreciate it.

230
00:11:16.409 --> 00:11:18.450
Next, we have the camera body itself.

231
00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:19.830
That's this right here.

232
00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:22.570
This is the Ursa Mini G2 camera.

233
00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:27.009
Again, there are a series of connectors here on the side.

234
00:11:27.009 --> 00:11:29.330
You can see that we have SDI out,

235
00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:31.830
we have SDI in and we have reference in

236
00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:33.429
and timecode in.

237
00:11:33.429 --> 00:11:36.830
These are where we're hooking up our connections

238
00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:40.389
from the ICV effects volume.

239
00:11:40.389 --> 00:11:42.990
So we have SDI out of the camera,

240
00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.789
which is leading into our Unreal control station

241
00:11:45.789 --> 00:11:48.950
through our Kona field recorder.

242
00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:51.990
We also have SDI out,

243
00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:55.549
which we really don't need that one,

244
00:11:55.549 --> 00:11:56.909
or excuse me, SDI in.

245
00:11:56.909 --> 00:11:59.029
We really don't need SDI in here.

246
00:11:59.029 --> 00:12:01.309
That's actually hooked up incorrectly.

247
00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:05.830
And then we have reference in and timecode in.

248
00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:10.830
So actually, we're gonna go ahead and remove this one.

249
00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:14.250
So you can see that we have genlock

250
00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:17.070
and we also have one for timecode.

251
00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:20.500
When we are working,

252
00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:25.019
this particular camera has only one port for that

253
00:12:25.019 --> 00:12:28.740
and it's labeled as reference in and timecode in.

254
00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:34.000
So you can only have one cable hooked up at a time.

255
00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:38.919
This is not something that's desirable,

256
00:12:38.919 --> 00:12:41.200
but it's not something that will stop you

257
00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:45.320
because this particular camera can still maintain timecode

258
00:12:45.320 --> 00:12:48.600
once you initially feed it timecode once.

259
00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:51.519
It's a process of what we call jamming timecode

260
00:12:51.519 --> 00:12:53.720
and we'll get into that in just a moment.

261
00:12:53.720 --> 00:12:56.480
But once you've jammed timecode into the camera

262
00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:01.440
and it's locked in with your house timecode,

263
00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:04.519
you can then plug in the genlock cable

264
